(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-pd2-exam-1-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"PD2 Exam 1 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A tympanic membrane perforation that does NOT extend to the margin is called what?","choices":["Attic","Traumatic","Central","Marginal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The classification is simply by whether the margin is involved, and a marginal perforation is the one that does involve it. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"HORIZONTAL diplopia — images side by side — points to a palsy of which cranial nerves?","choices":["Third or fourth","Fourth or sixth","Second or seventh","Third or sixth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and this is one to commit to memory. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"If a visual deficit is NOT corrected by pinhole testing, what should be considered?","choices":["Blepharitis","Cataract, optic nerve disease, or retinal disease","Simple myopia or astigmatism","Dry eye"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three when the problem is not refractive. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A bulla ruptures and leaves a moist area that does not bleed. How should this be described?","choices":["An ulcer","A fissure","An excoriation","An erosion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the moist, non-bleeding surface indicates the dermis has not been breached. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Lateral sparseness of the eyebrows suggests what?","choices":["Hyperthyroidism","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Iron deficiency","Hypothyroidism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the association for lateral eyebrow loss. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Endemic goitre is associated with what?","choices":["Thyroid-stimulating antibodies","Previous radiation exposure","Iodine deficiency","Autoimmune destruction of the gland"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Endemic means it clusters geographically, and the reason is dietary iodine. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which three systemic diseases did Beck single out as most relevant, and why the third?","choices":["Diabetes, hypertension and asthma","Rheumatoid arthritis, lupus and psoriasis","Thyroid disease, anaemia and epilepsy","Diabetes, hypertension and human immunodeficiency virus — because the last will affect basically any aetiology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — her reasoning for including it alongside the two vascular ones. — Lecture recording, 26 August 2026"},{"q":"Injection localised JUST AROUND THE CORNEA suggests which three conditions?","choices":["Keratitis, iritis, or acute glaucoma","Conjunctivitis, blepharitis, or dry eye","Subconjunctival haemorrhage, pinguecula, or pterygium","Episcleritis, chalazion, or hordeolum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the three, and this is the pattern that must not be dismissed as conjunctivitis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"After a chemical or fluid splash to the eye, what must you establish?","choices":["Whether the patient was wearing contact lenses","The manufacturer of the product","The acidity or alkalinity of the fluid","The volume of fluid involved"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is knowing this essential. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Scaling, sharply demarcated round plaques with central clearing describe which infection?","choices":["Tinea pedis","Tinea capitis","Tinea cruris","Tinea corporis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the ring with a clear centre is what gives ringworm its common name. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which cranial nerves carry referred pain to the ear?","choices":["XI and XII","V, VII, IX and X","III, IV and VI","VIII alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Four nerves supply sensation to the ear, which is exactly why disease well away from the ear can present as otalgia. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A generalised or diffuse distribution most commonly suggests which category of disease?","choices":["A fungal infection","A malignancy","A pressure injury","An allergic reaction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a process delivered through the bloodstream reaches the whole skin surface. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What must be done if part of the examination was not performed?","choices":["Leave the section blank without further comment","Obtain the finding from a classmate who examined the same patient","Document why it was not done — findings may never be invented","Record the finding that would be expected if it were normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recording something you did not do is the one error the guidance treats as absolute. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 20"},{"q":"An eruption confined to a single dermatome should suggest which diagnosis?","choices":["Herpes zoster","Psoriasis","Intertrigo","Tinea capitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — dermatomal distribution is the defining feature of zoster. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What should the opening statement of an oral case presentation include?","choices":["The diagnosis and the proposed treatment plan","The complete review of systems, system by system","The laboratory and imaging results obtained so far","The past medical history and the chief complaint"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — those two orient the listener before any detail follows. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 9"},{"q":"Warts are caused by which organism?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Human papillomavirus","Herpes simplex virus","A dermatophyte fungus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — verrucae are small harmless tumors of the skin, grey to flesh coloured, sometimes with rough hornlike projections. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A patient's eye pain is relieved by a topical anaesthetic. What does that suggest, and what does the opposite suggest?","choices":["Relief suggests a deep source; no relief suggests a surface problem","Relief confirms acute glaucoma","The response tells you nothing about depth","Relief suggests a surface problem such as a corneal injury"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is the response as a depth test. Pain NOT relieved suggests a deeper source. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How is nasal patency tested?","choices":["Occlude one nostril and ask the patient to breathe in","Ask the patient to blow the nose and listen for asymmetry","Hold a mirror under the nose and compare the two areas of condensation","Transilluminate each nostril in a dark room"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each side is tested separately, because unilateral obstruction is what carries the worrying differential. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Tinnitus WITH hearing loss and vertigo together points to which diagnosis?","choices":["Ménière disease","Benign positional vertigo","Acute labyrinthitis","Otitis externa"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tinnitus with hearing loss AND vertigo together is Ménière; it is the combination that makes the diagnosis, not any one of the three. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"In which three situations should the pupils NOT be dilated?","choices":["If visual acuity is reduced in either eye","If serial neurologic examinations are required, in elderly patients who have had cataract surgery, and if acute angle-closure glaucoma is suspected","In any patient over sixty-five, in diabetics, and in contact lens wearers","If the patient has conjunctivitis, blepharitis or dry eye"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three contraindications. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What does a HAZY cornea in a suspected abrasion suggest?","choices":["Bacterial infection should be suspected","The abrasion is healing normally","The fluorescein was applied incorrectly","Raised intraocular pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is haziness as pointing to infection. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"A firm raised lesion at an old ear piercing site. Which finding is it, and who is most prone to it?","choices":["Basal cell carcinoma — more common in darker-skinned individuals","A keloid — scar tissue, more common in darker-skinned individuals","Tophi — and there is no skin-type association","A cutaneous cyst — more common in darker-skinned individuals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Keloids are commonest on the shoulders and upper chest but arise at piercing sites, which puts them on the ear. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which component of the triple response of Lewis is produced by transudation of fluid?","choices":["The formation of a linear wheal","The initial red line","The reflex flare with broadening erythema","The fading of the lesion after several minutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — fluid leaving the vessels creates the raised, edematous wheal. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What should you do if nystagmus is seen on lateral gaze?","choices":["Bring the finger back into binocular vision","Repeat the test with the other eye covered","Refer immediately regardless","Measure the intraocular pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — persistence within the binocular field is what makes it abnormal. If the nystagmus persists there, consider a neurologic condition. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"How should the scalp be examined?","choices":["Ask the patient to wet the hair before inspection","Separate the hair into sections in order to observe the scalp beneath","Inspect the hair from a distance without touching the patient","Palpate the scalp through the hair without visual inspection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the scalp cannot be assessed through intact hair. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What is the ABNORMAL finding on the swinging light test, and what is it called?","choices":["Failure of the consensual reflex with a normal direct reflex — an efferent defect","Both pupils constricting more than expected","The pupil failing to constrict on accommodation only","Paradoxical pupil DILATION to direct light with an intact consensual reflex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Beck stressed the afferent lesion is the optic nerve, and the name. A Marcus Gunn pupil, an afferent defect. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What does GENERALISED lymphadenopathy raise?","choices":["Thyroiditis and goitre","Human immunodeficiency virus","Dental infection and tonsillitis","Otitis media and otitis externa"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Generalised disease means a systemic process rather than drainage from one infected site. Epstein-Barr virus, lymphoma, leukaemia and sarcoidosis. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which features should be inspected in the nail beds?","choices":["Capillary refill time and nail bed temperature","Length, cleanliness and evidence of biting","Shape, size, colour, brittleness, hemorrhages, lines and grooves, clubbing and pitting","Shape and colour only, since the remainder require specialist assessment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — eight features, each of which can point toward a systemic diagnosis. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Why should recent dental work be asked about when a patient presents with facial pain?","choices":["Dental work is a recognised cause of epistaxis","Dental infection is the commonest cause of frontal sinusitis","Dental work may affect the maxillary sinuses","Dental anaesthetic commonly causes anosmia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The maxillary sinus floor sits directly above the upper tooth roots, so a dental procedure can present as sinus pain. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the pattern of redness in conjunctivitis, and where is it maximal?","choices":["Diffuse dilation of the conjunctival vessels","Redness maximal around the cornea, sparing the periphery","A single sharply demarcated red patch","A violaceous discolouration of the sclera"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — peripheral maximum is what separates it from a ciliary pattern. With redness maximal PERIPHERALLY. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Where are the lesions of Kaposi's sarcoma typically distributed?","choices":["Confined to sun-exposed areas of the face","Confined to a single dermatome","Confined to the skin creases and folds","Widely disseminated on the legs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the wide dissemination is part of what makes the diagnosis recognisable. Trunk, arms, neck and head. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which statement about pruritus is correct?","choices":["It is a sensation that causes the desire to scratch and is not itself a diagnosis","It is a diagnosis in its own right when no rash is visible","It always indicates an allergic cause","It is only clinically significant when it disturbs sleep"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treating pruritus as a diagnosis is the error being warned against. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the test of whether an oral case presentation was a good one?","choices":["It leads the listener to the same differential the presenter formulated","It lasts less than two minutes from start to finish","It includes every element contained in the written note","It avoids all medical terminology in favour of plain language"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a functional test rather than a stylistic one, and the sharpest single criterion given. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 9"},{"q":"What causes a PERFORATED nasal septum?","choices":["Trauma, surgery or drug use","Allergic rhinitis and chronic sinusitis","A foreign body lodged for several days","Repeated epistaxis from Kiesselbach's plexus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three destroy the septal blood supply or the cartilage itself, and drug use is the one patients do not volunteer. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which three structures are inspected and palpated before the otoscope is picked up?","choices":["The parotid gland, the temporomandibular joint and the zygomatic arch","The preauricular, postauricular and occipital nodes","The auricles, the mastoid and the tragus","The tympanic membrane, the ossicles and the cochlea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tenderness found here localises disease before anything is put into the canal, and tragal tenderness in particular points to the canal itself. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A purplish-blue non-blanching macule larger than one centimetre that fades over time is called what?","choices":["A petechia","A spider angioma","An ecchymosis","Purpura"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the change in colour over time reflects breakdown of extravasated blood. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What does the canal look like in ACUTE otitis externa?","choices":["Normal, with the abnormality confined to the tympanic membrane","Swollen, narrow, moist, pale and tender, and it may be erythematous","Thickened, red and itchy","Dry, scaly and pale, with no tenderness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The swelling and tenderness dominate, and the narrowing is often what makes the examination difficult. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which four elements make up the ocular history?","choices":["Fever, weight loss, night sweats and rash","Family history alone","Corrective lenses, acute or chronic eye problems, eye medications, and eye surgery history","Smoking, alcohol, occupation and hobbies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the four, with glaucoma, antiglaucoma drops and cataract removal as its examples. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A patient speaks unusually SOFTLY despite a hearing complaint. Which loss does that point to?","choices":["Sensorineural — the patient compensates by speaking quietly","Mixed loss only","Presbycusis specifically","Conductive — the inner ear and cochlear nerve are intact"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and bone conduction of their own voice is if anything enhanced, so they have no reason to speak up. So the patient hears their own voice normally. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which four features make up Horner syndrome?","choices":["Ptosis, mydriasis, lateral deviation, and a fixed pupil","Lid lag, proptosis, chemosis and diplopia","A large slow pupil with reduced light reaction and slow near reaction","Ptosis, miosis of the affected eye, anhidrosis of the ipsilateral face"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and the preserved reaction is what separates it from a third nerve palsy. A small pupil that still reacts to light and near. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"On the red-eye chart, which column has NO ocular discharge AND an unaffected pupil AND a clear cornea AND absent pain?","choices":["Glaucoma","Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Conjunctivitis","Acute iritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — every column reads negative, which is what makes it benign. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"Which two situations can the tuning fork tests NOT distinguish?","choices":["Normal hearing from bilateral sensorineural loss, and normal hearing from mixed conductive and sensorineural loss","Conductive from sensorineural loss","Right-sided from left-sided loss","Presbycusis from noise-induced loss"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both tests work by comparing sides or routes, so a loss that is symmetrical or that affects both routes leaves the comparison looking normal. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A patient says others mumble, and that they struggle most in a noisy restaurant. Which kind of hearing loss does that pattern suggest?","choices":["Sensorineural","Conductive — noise masks the residual hearing","Mixed loss, which always presents this way","Neither — this is a description of tinnitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pattern is loss of discrimination rather than loss of volume, and background noise competes with what little discrimination remains. Trouble understanding speech, and worse in noise. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which examination comes FIRST in the suggested order?","choices":["Visual acuity","Direct ophthalmoscopy","Ocular pressure","Inspection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the order begins with inspection. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How is transillumination of the MAXILLARY sinus performed?","choices":["Shine the light into the nostril and look at the forehead","In a dark room","In a dark room, shine the light up under the brow close to the nose","In a bright room, shine the light through the cheek from the side"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The mouth must be open because the glow is looked for on the hard palate. Shine the light down just below the inner corner of the eye with the patient's mouth open. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is interrupted in Horner syndrome?","choices":["The oculomotor nerve","The optic nerve","The sympathetic innervation of the pupil and the levator palpebrae superioris","The parasympathetic supply through the ciliary ganglion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which explains both the small pupil and the ptosis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What are the fundoscopic findings in glaucomatous cupping?","choices":["The physiologic cup is enlarged","A pink swollen disc with blurred margins","A white disc with absent vessels","A cherry-red spot at the macula"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the description. More than half the disc diameter, with retinal vessels sinking in and around the disc. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Fine scale that is attached at the periphery and detached at the centre, seen at the edge of an inflammatory lesion, is called what?","choices":["Crust","Excoriation","Collarette scale","Lichenification"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — pityriasis rosea is the condition in which it is classically seen. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What does ABSENCE of a glow on sinus transillumination indicate, and how much weight should it carry?","choices":["Thickened mucosa or secretions, and it is diagnostic of sinusitis","A normal air-filled sinus","A deviated septum","Thickened mucosa or secretions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the caveat is the important half. A negative or positive result should not move the diagnosis far on its own. But the test is neither sensitive nor specific. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How is an atrophic scar described?","choices":["Rough with accentuated skin lines","Thin and white","Thick and pink","Raised and extending beyond the wound"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — as opposed to a hypertrophic scar, which is thick and pink. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A diffusely enlarged, FIRM thyroid points to what?","choices":["A multinodular goitre","Thyroid carcinoma","Hashimoto thyroiditis","Graves disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Firmness is the feature that separates it from Graves, where the gland is soft. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Partial thickness skin loss involving the epidermis, dermis or both defines which stage of pressure ulcer?","choices":["Stage II","Stage I","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — partial thickness is what distinguishes stage II from the full thickness loss of stage III. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which term refers to a tinea infection of the groin?","choices":["Tinea unguium","Tinea cruris","Tinea corporis","Tinea capitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — cruris denotes the groin. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which set of questions forms the core of a proper dermatological history?","choices":["The results of any previous biopsy, culture and blood work","Where the problem first appeared, what it looked like, how it has progressed, associated symptoms, and what treatment has been tried","The patient's age, occupation, marital status and insurance carrier","Whether the lesion is a macule, papule, plaque or nodule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those five questions cover onset, original appearance, evolution, symptoms and prior therapy. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which term describes lesions that remain separate from one another?","choices":["Confluent","Grouped","Reticular","Discrete"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — discrete lesions stay distinct rather than merging. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is the correct technique for everting the upper lid to search for a foreign body?","choices":["Press on the globe itself to push the lid outward","Use a cotton bud placed at the lid margin itself","Ask the patient to look down and relax","Ask the patient to look up, then pull the lid upward directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the step-by-step, including the 1 cm landmark. Raise the lid slightly so the lashes protrude, grasp the lashes and pull down and forward, then place a stick at least 1 cm above the lid margin at the upper border of the tarsal plate and push down as you raise the lid edge. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which statement about discharge in acute sinusitis is correct?","choices":["Discharge is absent in acute sinusitis","Purulent discharge is suggestive","Green discharge is diagnostic of bacterial infection","Clear discharge excludes sinusitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the distinction matters — patients and clinicians both over-read green discharge as proof of bacterial infection. But the COLOUR of the discharge is not diagnostic. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which term describes lesions involving the skin creases and folds?","choices":["Palmar-plantar","Intertriginous","Extensor","Photodistributed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the axillae, groin and inframammary folds are the classic intertriginous sites. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What kind of pressure is applied to the bulb during pneumatic otoscopy?","choices":["As hard as the patient will tolerate","A series of rapid repeated squeezes","Quick, firm but gentle","Slow and sustained, held for several seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Only a small pressure change is needed; the membrane's movement is slight, and force adds nothing but discomfort. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A palpable, elevated, plateau-like solid mass larger than one centimetre that occupies a large area compared with its elevation is called what?","choices":["A tumor","A patch","A plaque","A nodule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — psoriasis is the example, and a plaque may be coalesced papules. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 35"}]};
